"[2608.13696] Over the Memory Wall, Into the Instruction Wall: The New Bottleneck in GPU Data Processing (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.13696)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.13696\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 165\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Databases\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Aug 2026]\nL9: # Title:Over the Memory Wall, Into the Instruction Wall: The New Bottleneck in GPU Data Processing\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Sven Hepkema】, 【6†Bowen Wu】, 【7†Christos Kozyrakis】, 【8†Yannis Chronis】, 【9†Gustavo Alonso】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Over the Memory Wall, Into the Instruction Wall: The New Bottleneck in GPU Data Processing, by Sven Hepkema and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【10†View PDF】 【11†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Datacenter GPUs have seen an order-of-magnitude increase in memory bandwidth with the adoption of newer generations of HBM. Meanwhile, GPU database systems are gaining traction, many building on cuDF, an open-source library of GPU relational operators. Previously, query performance was bound by memory bandwidth, but the increase in memory bandwidth has not resulted in a proportional speedup of cuDF kernels.\nL17: To investigate why performance has not kept up, we built Valk, a performance analysis tool that combines data from multiple profilers. We profile cuDF running TPC-H in-memory on two extremes of hardware capability, the L4 and GH200 GPUs. The GH200 has 13.4$\\times$ the memory bandwidth and 2.5$\\times$ the instruction throughput of the L4, yet is only 5.2$\\times$ faster in running TPC-H. Our analysis shows that when memory bandwidth is increased, kernels become compute bound.\nL18: From our analysis, we make three recommendations to fully utilize the GPUs' potential for relational workloads when the memory wall is removed: kernels need to 1) make more efficient use of caches, and 2) increase occupancy and/or instruction level parallelism, and 3) execute fewer instructions per memory access.\nL19: Subjects:  | Databases (cs.DB)\nL20: Cite as:  | 【12†arXiv:2608.13696】 [cs.DB]\nL21:    | (or 【13†arXiv:2608.13696v1】 [cs.DB] for this version)\nL22:    | 【14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.13696†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Sven Hepkema [【15†view email】]\nL26: [v1] Thu, 13 Aug 2026 18:41:04 UTC (713 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Over the Memory Wall, Into the Instruction Wall: The New Bottleneck in GPU Data Processing, by Sven Hepkema and 4 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * 【10†View PDF】\nL35:   * 【11†HTML (experimental)】\nL36:   * 【16†TeX Source】\nL37: \nL38: 【17†view license】\nL39: \nL40: ### Additional Features\nL41: \nL42:   * 【18†Audio Summary】\nL43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.DB\nL46: \nL47: 【19†< prev】   |   【20†next >】\nL48: \nL49: 【21†new】 | 【22†recent】 | 【23†2026-08】\nL50: \nL51: Change to browse by:\nL52: \nL53: 【24†cs】\nL54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * 【25†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL58:   * 【26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL59:   * 【27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL64: \nL65: [Button: ×]\nL66: \nL67: loading...\nL68: \nL69: Data provided by:\nL70: \nL71: ### Bookmark\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL74: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL75: \nL76: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL77: \nL78: Bibliographic Explorer (【28†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL79: \nL80: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL81: \nL82: Connected Papers (【29†What is Connected 